<BaseLayout title="Methodology — Search Data Bench" description="How Search Data Bench tests search, SEO and AI data APIs.">
<section class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p><h1>How we test search, SEO and AI data APIs</h1><p class="lede">Documentation can prove that a vendor describes a capability. It cannot prove how that capability performed in our test.</p>
<h2>Evidence grades</h2><p><strong>VERIFIED HANDS-ON</strong> means we executed or directly observed the stated behavior and retained evidence.</p><p><strong>OFFICIAL DOCUMENTED</strong> means a current primary provider source supports the statement, but we did not execute that behavior in the stated test.</p><p><strong>THIRD-PARTY</strong> identifies outside evidence. <strong>NOT TESTED</strong> means our stated test did not execute it. <strong>UNAVAILABLE / LIMIT</strong> records an attempted frozen task blocked by an observed constraint. <strong>UNRESOLVED</strong> means the available evidence is insufficient to make the claim.</p>
<h2>Frozen inputs</h2><p>We design comparisons before seeing results. Query sets, market, language, device and evaluation rules are frozen. We do not replace inconvenient keywords or missing results after execution.</p>
<h2>No fake metric equivalence</h2><p>Difficulty, authority and trust metrics can use different datasets and formulas. We retain provider labels and compare observable disagreement instead of averaging them into a universal score.</p>
<h2>Missing data is evidence</h2><p>A missing result is not zero. A plan limit is not a product-wide absence. A delayed result is not automatically a failed feature.</p>
<h2>Commercial independence</h2><p>Affiliate links may fund this site. They do not change evidence grades, fill missing values or determine a benchmark result. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.</p></section>
</BaseLayout>